# 9. Insert Multiple Records

We don't always have to execute one `INSERT` for every student.

We can insert multiple rows in one statement.

```sql
INSERT INTO students
    (student_id, student_name, age, department)
VALUES
    (103, 'Arun', 20, 'CSE'),
    (104, 'Priya', 22, 'EEE'),
    (105, 'Kiran', 21, 'ECE');
```

Now our table contains:

| student_id | student_name | age | department |
| ---------: | ------------ | --: | ---------- |
|        101 | Ravi         |  20 | CSE        |
|        102 | Sita         |  21 | ECE        |
|        103 | Arun         |  20 | CSE        |
|        104 | Priya        |  22 | EEE        |
|        105 | Kiran        |  21 | ECE        |

This is useful when inserting multiple known records.

---

# 10. Text Values Need Quotes

When inserting text, we normally use single quotes.

Correct:

```sql
INSERT INTO students
VALUES (106, 'Rahul', 20, 'CSE');
```

Here:

```text
'Rahul'
'CSE'
```

are text values.

Numbers do not need quotes:

```text
106
20
```

So remember:

```text
Text   → 'Ravi'
Number → 20
```

---

# 11. INSERT with Different Data Types

Suppose we have:

```sql
CREATE TABLE employees (
    employee_id INT,
    employee_name VARCHAR(100),
    salary DECIMAL(10,2),
    joining_date DATE
);
```

We can insert:

```sql
INSERT INTO employees
    (employee_id, employee_name, salary, joining_date)
VALUES
    (101, 'Ravi', 45000.50, '2026-09-25');
```

Notice:

```text
101        → number
'Ravi'     → text
45000.50   → decimal
'2026-09-25' → date representation
```

---

# 12. What Happens if We Don't Provide a Column?

Suppose our table has:

```text
student_id
student_name
age
department
email
```

But we only provide:

```sql
INSERT INTO students
    (student_id, student_name, age, department)
VALUES
    (107, 'Anil', 22, 'CSE');
```

What happens to `email`?

That depends on the column definition.

If the column allows missing values and has no default value, it can be stored as:

```text
NULL
```

This connects to what we learned in Phase 0.

---

# 13. INSERT NULL

Suppose we don't know a student's phone number.

We can explicitly insert `NULL`:

```sql
INSERT INTO students
    (student_id, student_name, age, department, phone)
VALUES
    (108, 'Meena', 21, 'ECE', NULL);
```

Here:

```text
phone = NULL
```

means the phone value is unknown or unavailable.

Remember:

```text
NULL ≠ 0
NULL ≠ ''
```

