# 26. Important DML Safety Rule

Before running:

```sql
UPDATE
```

or:

```sql
DELETE
```

ask yourself:

> **Which rows will this statement affect?**

For example:

```sql
UPDATE students
SET department = 'CSE'
WHERE student_id = 101;
```

Only one student should be affected.

But:

```sql
UPDATE students
SET department = 'CSE';
```

may affect every student.

Similarly:

```sql
DELETE FROM students
WHERE student_id = 101;
```

removes one matching record.

While:

```sql
DELETE FROM students;
```

removes all records.

---

# 27. A Good Habit: Test the Condition First

Before executing:

```sql
UPDATE students
SET department = 'CSE'
WHERE age = 20;
```

first check which rows match the condition.

Later, we will learn `SELECT` in detail.

For now, the idea is:

```text
First understand the target rows
             ↓
Then UPDATE / DELETE
```

This habit becomes very important when working with production databases.

